    {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186064250,
        "id": "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7",
        "title": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "bacbded8-6884-5d02-af40-16c8adf1cd96",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4}{|5 x|\\sqrt{25 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1} \\left|{u}\\right|}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C$.\n\nWork on a connected interval where $|5 x|>1$.\n\nChoose the antiderivative. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=5 x$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(5)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork on a connected real interval where $|5 x|>1$.\n\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}u$. The outside factor is $4\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{4}{|5 x|\\sqrt{25 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{4}{5}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1} \\left|{u}\\right|}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1} \\left|{u}\\right|}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{4}{5}\\left(\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}\\left(\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(5 x \\right)}\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "cbb9e342-9d9a-5b33-a07b-d9352e99a26a",
